This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used**:
SDK `11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128`, runtime `Microsoft.NETCore.App 11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128`, macOS arm64. `<LangVersion>15.0</LangVersion>`, `net11.0`.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

`repro.csproj`:

```xml
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>15.0</LangVersion>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
```

`Program.cs`:

```cs
Console.WriteLine(Pick(new A("a")));

static string Pick(Choice value)
{
    switch (value)
    {
        case A a: return a.Text;
        case B b: return b.Message;
        case null: throw new InvalidOperationException();
    }
}

public sealed record A(string Text);
public sealed record B(string Message);
public readonly union Choice(A, B);
```

Run `dotnet run -c Debug`, then `dotnet run -c Release`.

**Expected Behavior**:
Both configurations print `a`. The method compiles without diagnostics, so the compiler treats the end of the switch as unreachable.

**Actual Behavior**:
Debug prints `a`. Release throws before the method runs its first branch:

```
Unhandled exception. System.InvalidProgramException: Common Language Runtime detected an invalid program.
   at Program.<<Main>$>g__Pick|0_0(Choice value)
   at Program.<Main>$(String[] args)
```

The Release failure happens only when all of these hold:
- the method is synchronous (non-async);
- its last top-level statement is a `switch` **statement** over a union value;
- the switch is exhaustive through union case types plus `case null`, with no `default`;
- every arm returns or throws.

The return type doesn't matter: `string`, the union itself and another union type all fail.

Each of these variants runs correctly in Release:
- the same logic written as a `switch` expression;
- the same switch with `default:` instead of `case null:`;
- a `throw` placed after the switch;
- the method made `async`;
- the switch inside a `try`/`finally` or a loop, or nested in an `if`/`else` with code after it;
- arms that assign a local and `break`, followed by `return`;
- `void` methods;
- an `if` chain over `value.Value`;
- switching on `object` instead of the union.

This looks like the other side of #85265. Without `case null`, reachability analysis reports CS0161/CS0177. With `case null`, the method compiles, but the Release IL seems to fall off the end of the method.

**Workaround**: use a switch expression, or add `default: throw new UnreachableException();`.

**Version Used:** dotnet/roslyn `main`

**Steps to Reproduce:**
```csharp
var source = """
    #nullable enable
    using System;
    public class PriorityAttribute : Attribute
    {
        public PriorityAttribute(string name) { }
    }
    public class Sample
    {
        private const string Value = "x";
        public void Method() { }
    }
    """;
var comp = CSharpCompilation.Create("c", new[] { CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText(source) }, ...);
var tree = comp.SyntaxTrees.Single();
var model = comp.GetSemanticModel(tree);

var method = tree.GetRoot().DescendantNodes().OfType<MethodDeclarationSyntax>().Single();

// Detached, freshly-built AttributeSyntax, matching Roslyn's own SpeculativeModel_InAttribute01 test.
var speculativeAttribute = SyntaxFactory.Attribute(
    SyntaxFactory.ParseName("Priority"),
    SyntaxFactory.ParseAttributeArgumentList("(Value)"));

model.TryGetSpeculativeSemanticModel(method.SpanStart, speculativeAttribute, out var speculativeModel);
var argument = speculativeAttribute.ArgumentList!.Arguments[0].Expression;
var typeInfo = speculativeModel.GetTypeInfo(argument);
// typeInfo.Nullability.FlowState is None. Expected: NotNull (Value is a non-null const string).
```

**Diagnostic Id:** N/A (API behavior, not a diagnostic)

**Expected Behavior:** `Nullability.FlowState = NotNull`.

**Actual Behavior:** `Nullability.FlowState` is `None`.

**Root Cause:**

`AttributeSemanticModel.IsNullableAnalysisEnabledCore()` does:
```csharp
return IsNullableAnalysisEnabledIn(Compilation, (AttributeSyntax)Root);
```
`Root` here is the detached node passed to `TryGetSpeculativeSemanticModel` — its `.SyntaxTree` is a synthetic tree scoped to just that node's own text, blind to any `#nullable enable` pragma outside that window, so the lookup falls back to the compilation-wide default.

`SyntaxTreeSemanticModel.CreateSpeculativeAttributeSemanticModel` already computes the correct answer via `IsNullableAnalysisEnabledAtSpeculativePosition`, but only uses it to decide whether to fetch a member model — the result is never passed to the model it constructs, which independently recomputes (and gets wrong) the same question.

**Proposed Fix** (for illustration, not a final PR against `main`): https://github.com/martin-strecker-sonarsource/roslyn/pull/5

Calls `IsNullableAnalysisEnabledAtSpeculativePosition` directly from `IsNullableAnalysisEnabledCore()` — the same helper `CreateSpeculativeAttributeSemanticModel` already calls separately to decide whether to fetch a member model — in place of the broken directive-lookup fallback. This doesn't reuse the earlier call's result; it calls the helper a second time. Validated with zero regressions against `NullableReferenceTypesTests.cs` (4488 passed) and `NullablePublicAPITests`/`SemanticModelGetSemanticInfoTests` (587 passed). The branch is stacked on a reproducer-only PR for review convenience; the actual fix is a small, self-contained diff.

Can you confirm this is indeed a bug, and would a PR implementing a fix along these lines be welcome?



