Sibling issue: https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/85807

## Version Used

- `Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp.Workspaces` 5.0.0 and 5.9.0 (latest stable on NuGet as of 2026-09-28)
- Reproduced with a plain `AdhocWorkspace`, target `net10.0`, on Windows

## Steps to Reproduce

`ImportAdder.AddImportsAsync` always terminates the *added* `using` directive with `\r\n`
when the target document contains **no line break at all**, no matter how the desired
newline is configured: passed as the `options` argument, set on `Solution.WithOptions`,
or set via `.editorconfig`'s `end_of_line`.

```csharp
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Editing;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Formatting;
using Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Text;
using OptionSet = Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Options.OptionSet;

// Document with NO line break at all, and one fully-qualified type reference
// that ImportAdder will shorten by adding a using directive.
const string source = "class C { global::System.Text.StringBuilder F() => null; }";

async Task<string> RunAsync(bool viaOptionSetArg, bool viaSolutionWithOptions, bool viaEditorConfig)
{
    var workspace = new AdhocWorkspace();
    var projectId = ProjectId.CreateNewId();
    var docId = DocumentId.CreateNewId(projectId);
    var projectInfo = ProjectInfo.Create(
        projectId, VersionStamp.Default, "Repro", "Repro", LanguageNames.CSharp,
        metadataReferences: [MetadataReference.CreateFromFile(typeof(object).Assembly.Location)]);

    var solution = workspace.CurrentSolution.AddProject(projectInfo);
    solution = solution.AddDocument(docId, "C.cs", SourceText.From(source));

    if (viaEditorConfig)
    {
        var configId = DocumentId.CreateNewId(projectId);
        solution = solution.AddAnalyzerConfigDocument(
            configId, ".editorconfig", SourceText.From("root = true\n[*.cs]\nend_of_line = lf\n"),
            filePath: @"C:\Repro\.editorconfig");
    }

    if (viaSolutionWithOptions)
    {
        solution = solution.WithOptions(
            solution.Options.WithChangedOption(FormattingOptions.NewLine, LanguageNames.CSharp, "\n"));
    }

    var document = solution.GetDocument(docId)!;

    OptionSet? optionSetArg = viaOptionSetArg
        ? document.Project.Solution.Options.WithChangedOption(FormattingOptions.NewLine, LanguageNames.CSharp, "\n")
        : null;

    var result = await ImportAdder.AddImportsAsync(document, optionSetArg);
    return (await result.GetTextAsync()).ToString();
}

// All four calls print the SAME result below.
Console.WriteLine(await RunAsync(viaOptionSetArg: false, viaSolutionWithOptions: false, viaEditorConfig: false));
Console.WriteLine(await RunAsync(viaOptionSetArg: true,  viaSolutionWithOptions: false, viaEditorConfig: false));
Console.WriteLine(await RunAsync(viaOptionSetArg: false, viaSolutionWithOptions: true,  viaEditorConfig: false));
Console.WriteLine(await RunAsync(viaOptionSetArg: false, viaSolutionWithOptions: false, viaEditorConfig: true));
```

## Expected Behavior

The inserted `using System.Text;` directive is terminated with `\n`, in every one of
the four scenarios above (`options` argument, `Solution.WithOptions`, and `.editorconfig`
`end_of_line = lf`), since each of these is a supported way to request `\n` as the
line ending.

## Actual Behavior

In all four scenarios the added `using` (and the blank line the adder inserts after it)
is terminated with `\r\n`, printed here with line endings made explicit:

```
usingSystem.Text;<CRLF><CRLF>class C { global::System.Text.StringBuilder F() => null; }
```

(The missing space after `using` is a separate cosmetic artifact of not running the
formatter afterwards and is not part of this report.)

Looking at the source, `AddImportHelpers.MoveTrivia` picks the new line ending like this
when the document has no existing `using`s (`src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Workspace/Core/LanguageServices/AddImports/AddImportHelpers.cs`):

```csharp
var endOfLine = root.DescendantTrivia().FirstOrNull((trivia, syntaxFacts) =>
    syntaxFacts.IsEndOfLineTrivia(trivia), syntaxFacts) ?? syntaxFacts.ElasticCarriageReturnLineFeed;
```

When the document contains no end-of-line trivia to copy from (our case: a document with
no line break at all), this unconditionally falls back to `ElasticCarriageReturnLineFeed`,
ignoring `AddImportPlacementOptions`/`LineFormattingOptions.NewLine` entirely. This is a
narrower, still-open case of #65081 ("`AddImportsAnnotation` doesn't match existing line
endings", fixed by #69309): that fix made the adder copy the line ending from the first
existing `using`/line in the file, and intentionally ignores `.editorconfig`'s
`end_of_line` when one exists to copy — but it did not add a fallback to the configured
`NewLine` option for the case where the file has *no* line ending to copy from at all,
so that case still hardcodes `\r\n`.

Separately (and worth noting as part of "actual behavior"): the public
`ImportAdder.AddImportsAsync(Document, OptionSet? options = null, ...)` overload ignores
its `options` parameter outright — `ImportAdder.cs` contains the comment "Since no public
options affect the behavior we can ignore options parameter and pass no fallback
options" — so there is currently no public, document-independent way to force `\n` for
this API at all; only `.editorconfig` reaches the underlying `AddImportPlacementOptions`,
and even that is bypassed in the no-line-break case described above.

## Context

Found while writing a code fix that inserts an attribute into files that may be
newly-created and empty (no line break yet); we work around it by post-processing the
returned document text to normalize line endings ourselves before handing it back to the
workspace.

